    <origin>Route authorization sweep 2026-10-02 (notes/route-authorization-sweep-2026-10-02.md, workflow run wf_c2daa097-2f4), slice admin-spe, findings #44, #45, #47, #48, #72, #73, #74, #75, #77. Every finding was confirmed by an independent skeptic; none is covered by an existing task (task 091 only moved ContainerItemEndpoints onto the /api/spe group). Owner round 9 (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md) binds: UAC-r2 fixes all 82, including surfaces other projects own; operator routes require an admin policy, not plain sign-in; fail closed, and an unknown id and a denied id get the same answer.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Two operator surfaces are under-protected. (1) The three /api/admin/record-matching routes check only that the caller is signed in, then run app-only Dataverse reads and AI Search writes over every matter, project and invoice. Put them behind the existing "SystemAdmin" policy that /api/admin/jobs uses. (2) The /api/spe group already requires the SPE admin role and has a tenant-scope filter that confines an admin to configs in their own business units, but that filter never runs on GET, PUT and DELETE /api/spe/configs/{id} or on the two bulk routes, POST /api/spe/configs accepts any business unit and any other unit's container type or app identity, and the filter allows the request when its own lookup fails. Make the filter run on those five, check the business unit and the app identity on create and update, make the filter fail closed, and make an unknown config and an out-of-scope config answer identically.</prompt>

  <amendments source="main session, 2026-10-03, owner rounds 9-10" binding="true">
    <!-- Read these before step 0. Each overrides anything below that contradicts it. -->
    <amendment>Owner decisions now run to round 10: re-read notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md rounds 9 and 10 on work/unified-access-control-r2 before step 0. They are newer than this POML.</amendment>
    <amendment>Do NOT rename or move FinanceAuthorizationFilter, and do not create a RecordAccessAuthorizationFilter or any other generalized copy of it: reuse it as it is. The rename is follow-up task 170, after the sweep tasks are integrated. Several sweep tasks reuse this filter in parallel, and a move in one of them would conflict with all the others.</amendment>
    <amendment>Task 167 (the every-route guard) runs IN PARALLEL with this task. If RouteAuthorizationGuardTests on your branch has no Pending waiver for your routes (167 not yet landed), do NOT add waivers, ledger entries or Permanent waivers, and do NOT add your files to GovernedFiles. Instead, add a table "Route authorization ledger input" to your task note, with one row per route you changed or deleted: the route key exactly as the guard spells it (VERB /path), the mechanism that now decides (filter, probe or impersonation), the deny test (its fully qualified name, proving a caller without rights is refused), and, for a list whose query runs AS the caller, the words "handler decision: query runs as the caller". The main session records these in 167's ledger at integration. If 167 HAS landed on your branch, follow its ledger contract: delete your Pending waivers and set ResolvedBy and ProofTest in the same diff.</amendment>
    <amendment>Owner round 10 item 1 (BINDING): delete a route when it has NO caller in the repo AND it is NOT in any published API description; fix it otherwise. Search the repo for OpenAPI documents and Copilot or agent plugin manifests (for example *openapi*, ai-plugin*, *manifest*.json, declarativeAgent*) before you decide. List every deletion, with its no-caller and not-published evidence, in the note and the PR. Delete the services, filters and options that only the deleted routes used. Keep the file census count in RouteAuthorizationGuardTests right if a whole endpoint file goes.</amendment>
    <amendment>Impersonation: never assign ServiceClient.CallerId or CallerAADObjectId outside Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseImpersonation.cs (task 160 adds the single SDK entry point there, plus an arch rule). For the Web API, impersonate through DataverseWebApiService's existing impersonateSystemUserId parameter (MSCRMCallerID). It refuses Guid.Empty (task 104).</amendment>
    <amendment>Also in scope (no deferral; reviewers of 163, 165 and 167): fix the SystemAdmin authorization policy's scope-substring branch (Infrastructure/DI/AuthorizationModule.cs, about lines 371-374: any delegated scope CONTAINING "admin" passes). Require only the intended admin signal, and prove every group that shares the policy still admits a real administrator and refuses a non-admin. Also fix: SpeAdminTenantScope failing open; the SPE environments and dashboard routes without business-unit scoping; and containertypes register sending an app-only token to a host the caller chooses.</amendment>
    <amendment>Live write probes in dev are a manual gate for the main session (the owner approves live steps by batch). Do not run them.</amendment>
  </amendments>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer: BFF authorization (ASP.NET Core Minimal API endpoint filters per ADR-008, named authorization policies, ProblemDetails per ADR-019) and the SPE admin plane (SpeAdminTenantScope, SpeAdminGraphService, BulkOperationService). Fail closed (ADR-003).</role>

  <goal>After this task:
(1) POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync, POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync-incremental and GET /api/admin/record-matching/status require the "SystemAdmin" policy. A signed-in caller without the Admin or SystemAdmin app role gets 403 and the sync service is never called. Their 500 responses carry `ProblemDetailsHelper.Explain(...)` (secret values redacted) instead of the raw `ex.Message`.
(2) GET, PUT and DELETE /api/spe/configs/{configId} are confined by SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter: a config whose business unit the caller cannot reach answers with exactly the same 404 as a config that does not exist, and no handler Dataverse read, write or delete of that config runs.
(3) POST /api/spe/configs requires a businessUnitId, and that business unit must be one the caller can reach. PUT /api/spe/configs/{configId} may only set businessUnitId to a business unit the caller can reach.
(4) POST /api/spe/configs, and PUT /api/spe/configs/{configId} when it changes the value, refuse a containerTypeId, owningAppId, keyVaultSecretName, consumingAppId or consumingAppKvSecret that another config already carries in a business unit the caller cannot reach.
(5) POST /api/spe/bulk/delete and POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions are confined by the same filter using the configId in the JSON body. An out-of-scope or unknown config answers with the same 404 and nothing is enqueued. In the background job, a container that is not of the config's container type is refused per item and never written.
(6) SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter reads configId from the query string, the route and the bound body, and answers 400 when two of them disagree, so no route authorizes one config and acts on another.
(7) SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter fails closed: if the config's business unit, or the business-unit hierarchy, cannot be read, the request is refused with 503 instead of passed through.
(8) Each of the nine routes passes RouteAuthorizationGuardTests once task 167 governs its file, and the Pending waivers task 167 added for them are deleted.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 LIVE. Every anchor below was re-read on work/unified-access-control-r2 at b18a7d321 on 2026-10-02. Line numbers drift; re-verify in step 0.

PART 1 — RECORD-MATCHING ADMIN ROUTES (sweep #47 medium, #48 medium, #77 low).
- `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/Admin/RecordMatchingAdminEndpoints.cs:13-15` builds the group `/api/admin/record-matching` with a bare `.RequireAuthorization()` and `.WithTags("Admin")`, nothing else. The BFF registers no DefaultPolicy or FallbackPolicy, so that means "any authenticated caller".
- Routes: `POST /sync` (:18, handler `TriggerBulkSync` :51-73), `POST /sync-incremental` (:27, handler `TriggerIncrementalSync` :78-104), `GET /status` (:37, handler `GetSyncStatus` :109-127). No endpoint filter and no in-handler check.
- Client: `IDataverseIndexSyncService` → `DataverseIndexSyncService` (`Services/RecordMatching/DataverseIndexSyncService.cs`), registered only when the flag is on (`Infrastructure/DI/AnalysisServicesModule.cs:1732-1741`). It reads Dataverse with the BFF's own TokenCredential (around :98-108) and writes to AI Search through `SearchClientFactory` with the admin key or managed identity (around :84-89, MergeOrUpload around :422). Both legs are app-only.
- Effect: any signed-in user can start a full app-only re-read of all matters, projects and invoices plus a MergeOrUpload into the shared records index, on demand and repeatably (cost and denial of service). `/status` discloses the index name and document counts. The caller never chooses record ids and receives no record content.
- The 500 paths put `ex.Message` straight into ProblemDetails `detail` (:70, :101, :124). `ProblemDetailsHelper.Explain(summary, ex)` (`Infrastructure/Errors/ProblemDetailsHelper.cs:85-92`) is the codebase's admin-facing replacement: summary + exception type + `Redact(ex.Message)` (:45-56, strips bearer tokens, JWTs and `client_secret=`-style values). It deliberately still shows a redacted cause to an admin; it is not a removal.
- Mapped only when `DocumentIntelligence:RecordMatchingEnabled` is true (`Infrastructure/DI/EndpointMappingExtensions.cs:322-326`). The flag is an off switch, not an authorization check; deployed smoke baselines (projects/sdap-bff-api-remediation-fix/baseline/endpoints-smoke.json) show the routes live in dev.
- THE POLICY TO USE: `"SystemAdmin"`, registered at `Infrastructure/DI/AuthorizationModule.cs:362-377`. It passes on the Admin or SystemAdmin app role (`roles` claim or IsInRole). It is the policy `/api/admin/jobs` (`Api/Admin/JobsEndpoints.cs:51-54`), `/api/admin/membership` (`Api/Admin/MembershipAdminEndpoints.cs:44-47`) and `/api/ai/rag/admin` (`Api/Ai/RagEndpoints.cs:161-163`) put on their GROUP with `.RequireAuthorization("SystemAdmin")`. ⚠️ The same policy also passes when the claim `http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/scope` CONTAINS the substring "admin" (:372-374). The BFF exposes only the delegated scope `user_impersonation` (`scripts/Register-EntraAppRegistrations.ps1:1372-1385`), so this is latent today. Do NOT change the policy in this task (three other groups share it); record the weakness in the task note.
- No shipped client calls these routes (grep of the repo for "record-matching/sync" and "record-matching/status" 2026-10-02: only the endpoint file, Phase2RecordMatchingTests, smoke baselines and project notes). They are operator routes, called by hand (projects/ai-spaarke-platform-enhancments-r3/tasks/003-populate-records-index.poml step "Call the sync endpoint").
- Existing tests: `tests/integration/Spe.Integration.Tests/Phase2RecordMatchingTests.cs` asserts 401 for an unauthenticated caller on the admin routes (:235, :274) and "not 404" for an authenticated one (:260, :287), plus an endpoint-existence census that treats anything but 404 as "exists" (:353-380). A 403 satisfies all of them, but confirm by running the suite.

PART 2 — SPE ADMIN TENANT SCOPE (sweep #73 medium, #45 high, #75 medium, #74 medium, #44 high, #72 medium).
The group: `Api/SpeAdminEndpoints.cs:27-35` applies `.RequireAuthorization()`, then `AddSpeAdminAuthorizationFilter()` (layer 1: Admin or SystemAdmin app role only; `Api/Filters/SpeAdminAuthorizationFilter.cs:106`, `:146-168`, deny code `sdap.access.deny.role_insufficient` :96), then `AddSpeAdminTenantScopeFilter()` (layer 2: which business units' configs the admin may reach). `MapConfigEndpoints` registers at `:42`, `MapBulkOperationEndpoints` at `:63`. Mapped unconditionally (`EndpointMappingExtensions.cs:415`).
THE ADMIN POLICY QUESTION FOR /api/spe. Layer 1 already requires the same two app roles the "SystemAdmin" policy checks, minus the policy's scope-substring branch. Adding `.RequireAuthorization("SystemAdmin")` to /api/spe would be redundant and would replace the filter's 403 ProblemDetails (reasonCode `sdap.access.deny.role_insufficient`) with the policy's empty 403, which the shipped client keys its "Spaarke administrator permission required" banner on (`src/solutions/SpeAdminApp/src/services/speApiClient.ts:123-129`, `:156-176`). So the /api/spe admin gate stays as it is. It is NOT enough by itself, because these operator routes act on a caller-chosen config or container, and the codebase defines business-unit scope as the boundary between admins (`SpeAdminTenantScope` remarks: "a customer administrator sits in a leaf unit"). That is why the tenant-scope filter must run on every one of them.
The boundary: `Services/SpeAdmin/SpeAdminTenantScope.cs` (concrete class, registered scoped at `Infrastructure/DI/SpeAdminModule.cs:93`, constructed over the app-only `DataverseWebApiClient`). `GetAccessibleBusinessUnitsAsync` (:67-79) returns the caller's own business unit plus its descendants, resolved from the token oid via an app-only systemuser query; `ResolveCallerBusinessUnitAsync` (:141-178) swallows its own faults and returns null, which yields an EMPTY set (deny everything). `LoadBusinessUnitHierarchyAsync` (:207-226) is NOT caught and throws. `CanAccessConfigAsync` (:91-127) is the per-config check. Its class remarks (:13-32) say the BFF reads Dataverse app-only, so Dataverse business-unit trimming never applies and this class is the only cross-customer boundary on the admin plane.
The filter: `Api/Filters/SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter.cs`. `InvokeAsync` (:70-103) calls `CanAccessConfigAsync` and answers 404 (:91-99: detail "Container type config '{configId}' was not found.", title "Not Found", extensions `errorCode` = "spe.admin.deny.config_out_of_scope" and `traceId`). `TryReadConfigId` (:114-129) reads the query key `configId`, and reads the route value named `configId` ONLY when the query value is empty. With neither present the request passes straight through (:76-79). It is the only caller of `CanAccessConfigAsync` in the codebase (grep 2026-10-02).

FIVE WAYS THE BOUNDARY IS BYPASSED TODAY:
(a) ROUTE PARAM NAMED "id". `Api/SpeAdmin/ConfigEndpoints.cs` maps `GET /{id:guid}` (:69, handler `GetConfigAsync` :198-252), `PUT /{id:guid}` (:83, `UpdateConfigAsync` :346-424) and `DELETE /{id:guid}` (:91, `DeleteConfigAsync` :430-494). The filter finds no `configId` and passes. None of the three handlers takes `SpeAdminTenantScope`. So any SPE admin, in any business unit, can read (including `sprk_keyvaultsecretname`, owning and consuming app ids), rewrite (including `sprk_BusinessUnit`, `sprk_containertypeid`, `sprk_owningappid`, `sprk_keyvaultsecretname`) or delete another business unit's config by GUID through the app-only `DataverseWebApiClient`. Re-pointing another unit's config to the caller's own business unit then makes every configId-scoped container route pass the filter for that config.
(b) CONFIGID IN THE JSON BODY. `Api/SpeAdmin/BulkOperationEndpoints.cs` maps `POST /bulk/delete` (:50, handler `EnqueueBulkDelete` :105-151) and `POST /bulk/permissions` (:65, handler `EnqueueBulkPermissions` :159-237). Both bind `[FromBody]` records that carry `ConfigId` as a STRING (`Models/SpeAdmin/BulkOperationModels.cs:32-34` `BulkDeleteRequest`, `:61-66` `BulkPermissionsRequest`). The filter never sees a body configId. The handlers check only shape (count, at most 500, GUID format :131-138 / :185-192, user XOR group, role allow-list) and enqueue. `Services/SpeAdmin/BulkOperationService.cs` (sealed `BackgroundService`, singleton + hosted at `SpeAdminModule.cs:112-113`) then runs with no caller context: `ProcessDeleteJobAsync` (:225-) calls `ResolveConfigAsync` (:246) and `GetClientForConfigAsync` (:257, app-only `ClientSecretCredential`, `Infrastructure/Graph/SpeAdminGraphService.cs:576`) and soft-deletes every caller-supplied container id (:280); `ProcessPermissionsJobAsync` (:328-) does the same (:349, :360) and grants the caller-chosen user or group a role up to "owner" (:382). These are the ONLY two request records in `Models/SpeAdmin` with a `ConfigId` property (grep 2026-10-02); every other /api/spe route passes `?configId=`.
(c) BUSINESS UNIT NOT INTERSECTED ON CREATE/UPDATE. `CreateConfigAsync` (:258-340) validates presence and Key Vault name format only; `BuildCreatePayload` (:535-568) binds `sprk_BusinessUnit` to whatever `BusinessUnitId` the caller sends (:561-562), and an omitted one creates a config with no business unit. `BuildUpdatePayload` (:570-600) does the same for PUT (:593-594). A POST carries no configId, so the filter passes it.
(d) APP IDENTITY NOT SCOPED ON CREATE/UPDATE (the core of finding #74). `containerTypeId`, `owningAppId`, `keyVaultSecretName`, `consumingAppId` and `consumingAppKvSecret` are written unchecked (`BuildCreatePayload` :540-551, `BuildUpdatePayload` :576-582). A config is the credential selector: `SpeAdminGraphService.ResolveConfigAsync` (:694-) reads the owning app id and secret name from it, and `GetClientForConfigAsync` (:576-) fetches that secret from the BFF Key Vault and builds an app-only Graph client. So an admin in unit A can create (or re-point) a config IN THEIR OWN UNIT that names unit B's container type or B's app and secret; every configId-scoped container, item, permission and recycle-bin route then passes the filter for it while acting app-only on B's containers. Business-unit intersection alone does not stop this.
(e) FAIL-OPEN SCOPE LOOKUP. `CanAccessConfigAsync` returns TRUE when reading the config's business unit throws (:101-116), and TRUE when the config does not exist OR has no business unit (:118-122; `ResolveConfigBusinessUnitAsync` :181-200 returns null for both). The comment's argument ("the endpoint must itself resolve the same config ... so an outage that defeats this lookup also defeats the endpoint") fails on a transient fault: the filter's read fails, the endpoint's own read a moment later succeeds, and a cross-business-unit configId gets through. After this task the scope decision guards five more routes, so this must fail closed.

ID SUBSTITUTION THE RENAME WOULD CREATE. After (a) is fixed by renaming the parameter to `{configId:guid}`, today's `TryReadConfigId` would authorize `?configId=Y` (the query wins) while the handler acts on the route value X. So the rename is only safe together with the "differing values → 400" rule.

WHY "UNKNOWN" MUST BE DENIED BY THE FILTER TOO. Today an unknown configId passes the filter (null business unit → true). For the config routes the handler then 404s with a DIFFERENT shape (`ConfigEndpoints.cs:219-223`, `:234-238`, `:381-385`, `:456-460`: extension `correlationId`, no `errorCode`) from the filter's out-of-scope 404 (`errorCode` + `traceId`). For the bulk routes an unknown config is accepted with 202 and fails silently in the job, while an out-of-scope one would 404. Either difference is an existence oracle. The fix distinguishes "does not exist" from "exists with no business unit" in `ResolveConfigBusinessUnitAsync` and has the FILTER return the one 404 for both "does not exist" and "out of scope".

THE BUSINESS-UNIT-LESS COMPATIBILITY RULE. `CanAccessConfigAsync`'s remarks (:84-90) record a deliberate choice: a config with NO business unit is accessible to every admin, so existing dedicated deployments keep working, and "every config MUST carry a business unit before a shared multi-customer environment is considered isolated". This task does not change that rule (escalation trigger 1). It stops NEW business-unit-less configs from being created (POST requires the field), and PUT already cannot clear the lookup (it binds only when a value is present).

REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION TO COPY for the business-unit intersection: `ListConfigsAsync` (`ConfigEndpoints.cs:121-192`), which derives the accessible set from the caller's token (`:132`), treats an empty set as "nothing" (`:134-142`), and intersects the caller's optional `businessUnitId` with that set instead of trusting it (`:144-153`). For reading a small table whole in one app-only query and deciding in memory, copy `SpeAdminTenantScope.LoadBusinessUnitHierarchyAsync` (:207-226, `top: 5000`).

CLIENT (shipped): `src/solutions/SpeAdminApp/src/services/speApiClient.ts` calls `/spe/configs` and `/spe/configs/{id}` (:447-487) and posts the bulk bodies with `configId` in the body and no query string (:1409-1424; `types/spe.ts:1103-1119`). The config form requires a business unit (`components/settings/ContainerTypeConfig.tsx:521` "Business Unit is required.") and its PUT sends EVERY field, unchanged ones included (`formStateToUpsert` :326-347) — so a per-field check on PUT must compare against the stored value, not fire on presence. Bulk delete is used by `components/search/ContainerResultsGrid.tsx:402`. Renaming the route parameter does not change any URL, and reading configId from the body keeps the client unchanged. `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/SpeAdminClientRouteAgreementTests.cs:401-402` collapses `{id}` / `{id:guid}` to `{}`, so the rename does not disturb the client-route agreement test.

TEST REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATIONS TO COPY:
- `tests/integration/auth/SpeAdmin/SpeAdminContainerItemRouteGateTests.cs`: a real-host HTTP test of the /api/spe group, with `SpeAdminRouteGateFixture` (:300-344) and `RoleHeaderAuthHandler` (:353-) choosing the caller's app roles per request (header `X-Test-App-Roles`, sentinel `(none)`; oid `0b7d1f60-9c3a-4d21-8f5e-2a6b7c8d9e01`), with positive, negative, anonymous and census controls. Files under `tests/integration/auth/**` compile into `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests` (`Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests.csproj:111`).
- `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/DelegationRuleTestFixture.cs:138-160`: substitutes the concrete `DataverseWebApiClient` with a Moq CLASS mock (`CallBase = false`; its `RetrieveAsync`, `CreateAsync`, `UpdateAsync`, `QueryAsync` are virtual: `src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseWebApiClient.cs:170`, `:184`, `:204`, `:227`). ⚠️ `DeleteAsync` (`:216`) is NOT virtual; making it virtual is the permitted seam for asserting "no delete ran".
- `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Api/Admin/AdminJobsTestFixture.cs` + `JobsEndpointsTests.cs:47` (`ListJobs_Returns403_WhenAuthenticatedButNotAdmin`) and `tests/integration/Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests/Admin/JobsEndpointsTests.cs:276` (`NonAdmin_Returns403_OnAllEndpoints`): the pattern for proving a "SystemAdmin" group refuses a non-admin.
- `tests/integration/contract/SpeAdmin/GraphWireMockFixture.cs` (`CreateGraphClient` :84, `StubGet` :103, `StubPost` :196, `StubDelete` :217, `RequestsFor` :260) with `SpeAdminContainerArchivalContractTests.cs:370-386` (`CreateSut`, constructing a real sealed `SpeAdminGraphService` with unusable credentials): the HTTP-fake pattern for driving Graph calls without Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;.

THE ROUTE GUARD. `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs` (task 074). `ConfigEndpoints.cs`, `BulkOperationEndpoints.cs` and `RecordMatchingAdminEndpoints.cs` are not in `GovernedFiles` today. The precedent for an /api/spe file is the `Scope.GroupGated` entry for `Api/SpeAdmin/ContainerItemEndpoints.cs` (:173-186; scope defined :104-121; enforced by `GroupGatedFilesRegisterNoAbsolutePaths` :1302-1348 and by the `RouteGroupBuilder` parameter of the Map method, which both `MapConfigEndpoints` and `MapBulkOperationEndpoints` already take). Owner round 9 item 3 has task 167 extend the guard to every route file, with each of the 82 findings starting as a Pending waiver owned by its fix task, and the stale-waiver rule (`NoWaiverIsStale` :987-, maintenance rule 3 at :359) forcing each fix to delete its waiver; `NoWaiverNamesARouteThatNoLongerExists` flags a waiver whose route template no longer exists. The guard checks the presence of a gate, not the tenant-scope semantics; the behavioural tests in this task are what prove the boundary holds. Task 167 had NO POML and no 165-owned waivers at authoring (2026-10-02).</background>
