  <escalation>
    <trigger>Business-unit-less configs. If the read-only count finds configs with no business unit in an environment with more than one customer business unit, or if closing any finding above turns out to require changing the "no business unit = accessible to every admin" rule in `CanAccessConfigAsync` (`SpeAdminTenantScope.cs:84-90`, `:118-122`): STOP and present options per CLAUDE.md §6 — (a) RECOMMENDED: keep the rule, require a business unit on create (this task), and file the backfill of existing rows as work; (b) deny business-unit-less configs to any admin whose business unit is not the root (breaks dedicated deployments whose admins sit in a child unit, owner round 5). Do not change the rule silently.</trigger>
    <trigger>A shipped consumer breaks. If any SpeAdminApp path, script or flow sends POST /api/spe/configs without a businessUnitId, sends a bulk request whose query and body configIds differ, relies on a 202 for an unknown config, or relies on a PUT that changes an identity field to one another business unit holds: STOP and name the caller. Do not relax the check to keep it working.</trigger>
    <trigger>An automated caller of /api/admin/record-matching/* (a pipeline, a scheduled script, an app-only token) holds neither the Admin nor the SystemAdmin role: STOP and report it. Do not widen the "SystemAdmin" policy and do not add an exception path.</trigger>
    <trigger>Per-container ownership. The per-item container-type check confines the bulk routes to the config's container type, but one container type can serve many customers (`docs/architecture/SPAARKE-SPE-CONTAINER-TYPE-TOPOLOGY.md` §3: Model 1 is one type with one container per customer). If the executor finds that closing finding #72 requires binding each container to the caller's business unit, and no authoritative container-to-business-unit mapping exists in Dataverse (check `businessunit.sprk_containerid` and the secure-record container columns read-only): STOP and present options rather than inventing a mapping.</trigger>
    <trigger>The fail-closed 503, or the filter's new does-not-exist 404, changes the outcome of an existing test or a shipped flow on any /api/spe route outside this task's six (all 68 routes share the filter): STOP and report the route and the test, rather than restoring the fail-open path or letting unknown configs through.</trigger>
    <trigger>Any fix appears to need an ADR exception (for example a per-handler authorization check against ADR-008 beyond the POST/PUT body checks this task allows, or a change to the shared "SystemAdmin" policy): STOP and raise it per CLAUDE.md §6.5 with the required format.</trigger>
    <trigger>Shared app identity. If the read-only count (item (e)) finds two or more configs with business units that are not all reachable by one leaf admin (that is, in different leaf units) carrying the same containerTypeId, owningAppId, keyVaultSecretName, consumingAppId or consumingAppKvSecret, so that the app-identity check would refuse a legitimate create or edit: STOP and present options per CLAUDE.md §6 — (a) RECOMMENDED: keep the check and have the owner confirm which rows are legitimate; (b) narrow the check to `containerTypeId` and `owningAppId` only. Do not drop the check silently.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Everything this task needs exists: the "SystemAdmin" policy (`Infrastructure/DI/AuthorizationModule.cs:362-377`, used by `JobsEndpoints.cs:53`, `MembershipAdminEndpoints.cs:46`, `RagEndpoints.cs:162`); the SPE admin role filter (`Api/Filters/SpeAdminAuthorizationFilter.cs`); the tenant-scope filter and its service (`Api/Filters/SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter.cs`, `Services/SpeAdmin/SpeAdminTenantScope.cs`, sole consumer of `CanAccessConfigAsync`); the caller-derived business-unit intersection (`ConfigEndpoints.cs:132-153`); the whole-table read shape (`SpeAdminTenantScope.LoadBusinessUnitHierarchyAsync` :207-226); the container read that returns a container type (`SpeAdminGraphService.GetContainerAsync`, :1281); the redacting error helper (`ProblemDetailsHelper.Explain`, :85) and the 403 helper (`ProblemDetailsHelper.Forbidden`, :155).</existing>
    <extension>Yes. No new filter, policy, service or DI registration. The work extends `SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter` (route value is reached by renaming the parameter; the bound body argument is read; disagreeing values are refused; the 404 shape is shared; faults deny) and `SpeAdminTenantScope` (exists versus no-business-unit; fault = unverifiable). Possible new types and members, each justified in the PR: a small result type for the scope decision (permitted / not found or out of scope / unverifiable), because a bool cannot carry the 503 case; a marker interface implemented by the two bulk request records so the filter finds a body configId by contract rather than by naming two concrete types — cost of doing without it: the next request record that carries a ConfigId in its body bypasses the boundary silently, exactly as the two bulk records did; one shared 404 helper, because four handler sites and the filter must produce a byte-identical answer; two internal per-item methods and one internal count on `BulkOperationService`, because the class is sealed and "no write was sent" / "nothing was enqueued" cannot otherwise be asserted deterministically.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Any signed-in user triggers full app-only Dataverse re-reads and AI Search writes over every matter, project and invoice on demand, and reads index metadata. Any SPE admin, in any business unit, reads another unit's config including its Key Vault secret name, rewrites it (including re-pointing its business unit to their own, which unlocks every configId-scoped container route for it), deletes it, creates configs with no business unit (visible to every admin), creates or re-points a config in their own unit that borrows another unit's container type or app and secret (unlocking that unit's containers app-only), and soft-deletes or grants owner on another unit's containers through the app-only Graph client. A transient Dataverse fault turns the tenant-scope check into an allow.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

