  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Record-matching, non-admin (findings #47 POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync, #48 POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync-incremental, #77 GET /api/admin/record-matching/status): through a real host with `DocumentIntelligence:RecordMatchingEnabled=true`, a signed-in caller whose token carries no Admin or SystemAdmin app role gets 403 on each of the three routes, and the substituted `IDataverseIndexSyncService` records ZERO calls. This holds for a caller with `roles=User` and for a caller with scope claim `user_impersonation` and no role.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Record-matching, admin: a caller with `roles=SystemAdmin`, and separately a caller with `roles=Admin`, reaches each of the three handlers (200 with the substituted service's result). An anonymous caller gets 401 on all three.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Record-matching, errors: when the substituted service throws an exception whose message contains a bearer token and a `client_secret=` value, each of the three routes returns 500 whose `detail` equals `ProblemDetailsHelper.Explain(&lt;that route's title&gt;, ex)` and contains neither secret value.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Record-matching, wiring: `RecordMatchingAdminEndpoints.cs` builds its group with `.RequireAuthorization("SystemAdmin")` and contains no bare `.RequireAuthorization()`; the "SystemAdmin" policy at `AuthorizationModule.cs:362-377` and `SpeAdminEndpoints.cs` are unchanged except as this task's constraints allow (diff shows no edit to the policy and no "SystemAdmin" policy on /api/spe).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">GET /api/spe/configs/{configId} (finding #73): for an admin caller whose accessible business units exclude the config's business unit, the response is 404 and is identical (status, title, detail text, `errorCode`, extension key set; trace id value aside) to the response for a configId that does not exist; the substituted DataverseWebApiClient records no `RetrieveAsync` on `sprk_specontainertypeconfigs` (only the scope `QueryAsync`). An in-scope config → 200 as today.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">GET, PUT and DELETE /api/spe/configs/{configId}, id substitution: a request whose route names config X while `?configId=` names a different, in-scope config Y → 400 with `errorCode` "spe.admin.deny.config_id_ambiguous", no `QueryAsync`, `RetrieveAsync`, `UpdateAsync` or `DeleteAsync` on `sprk_specontainertypeconfigs` is made.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">PUT /api/spe/configs/{configId} (finding #45): out-of-scope config → the same 404 as above and `UpdateAsync` is never called; in-scope config with a body `businessUnitId` the caller cannot reach (another unit, or a GUID that is no business unit) → one identical 403 with reasonCode "spe.admin.deny.business_unit_out_of_scope" and `UpdateAsync` is never called; in-scope config whose body changes `owningAppId` (and, as separate cases, `containerTypeId` and `keyVaultSecretName`) to a value another config in an unreachable business unit carries → 403 with reasonCode "spe.admin.deny.config_identity_out_of_scope" and `UpdateAsync` is never called; in-scope config with an in-scope or absent `businessUnitId` and identity fields equal to the stored values (the shipped client's full-body PUT) → 200 as today, even when another unreachable config shares one of those stored values.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">DELETE /api/spe/configs/{configId} (finding #75): out-of-scope config → the same 404 and `DeleteAsync` is never called; unknown config → the same 404 and `DeleteAsync` is never called; in-scope config → 204 as today.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">POST /api/spe/configs (finding #74), business unit: a body with no `businessUnitId` (or `Guid.Empty`) → 400 and no Dataverse call of any kind; a `businessUnitId` outside the caller's accessible set (another unit, or a GUID that is no business unit) → one identical 403 with reasonCode "spe.admin.deny.business_unit_out_of_scope" and `CreateAsync` is never called; a caller with an empty accessible set → the same 403; an in-scope `businessUnitId` with identity fields no unreachable config carries → 201 as today.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">POST /api/spe/configs (finding #74), app identity: with an in-scope `businessUnitId`, a body whose `containerTypeId` another config in an unreachable business unit carries → 403 with reasonCode "spe.admin.deny.config_identity_out_of_scope" and `CreateAsync` is never called; the same for `owningAppId`, `keyVaultSecretName`, `consumingAppId` and `consumingAppKvSecret` (five cases, matched case-insensitively after trimming); the 403 detail does not contain the other config's id or business unit; a value carried only by a config with NO business unit, or only by a config in a reachable unit, does not block (201); a fault on the identity read, or a read returning 5000 rows, → 503 with `errorCode` "spe.admin.deny.scope_unverifiable" and `CreateAsync` is never called.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">POST /api/spe/bulk/delete (finding #44) and POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions (finding #72): with `configId` only in the JSON body (the shipped client's shape), an out-of-scope config and a config that does not exist each get the same 404 as the config routes, the response is not 202, and `BulkOperationService`'s tracked-operation count is unchanged; an in-scope body configId → 202 as today and the count rises by one.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">POST /api/spe/bulk/delete and POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions, id substitution: a request whose query `?configId=` names an in-scope config while the body `configId` names a different config → 400 with `errorCode` "spe.admin.deny.config_id_ambiguous", no scope query is made on either value, and the tracked-operation count is unchanged; a query `?configId=` that is unparseable alongside a valid body configId → the same 400; a body configId that is unparseable with no query value → the handler's existing 400 (not 404).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">POST /api/spe/bulk/delete and POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions, per container: driving the delete per-item method and the permissions per-item method with the GraphWireMockFixture client and a real SpeAdminGraphService — (i) a container whose GET returns a different `containerTypeId` than the config's: the item is recorded as failed, and `RequestsFor` shows NO DELETE (delete path) and NO permissions POST (permissions path) for it; (ii) a container whose GET returns 404: the same per-item error text as (i) and no write; (iii) a container whose GET returns 500: the same per-item error text and no write; (iv) a container of the config's type, differing only in letter case of the type id: the write is sent as today. `ProcessDeleteJobAsync` and `ProcessPermissionsJobAsync` reach `SoftDeleteContainerAsync` / `GrantContainerPermissionAsync` only through these methods (grep of the file shows no other call site).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Filter fail-closed: when the substituted DataverseWebApiClient throws on the config business-unit query, or on the business-unit hierarchy query, each of the five filtered /api/spe routes (GET, PUT and DELETE /api/spe/configs/{configId}, POST /api/spe/bulk/delete, POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions) returns 503 with `errorCode` "spe.admin.deny.scope_unverifiable"; no `RetrieveAsync`, `CreateAsync`, `UpdateAsync` or `DeleteAsync` on `sprk_specontainertypeconfigs` is made and the tracked-operation count is unchanged. A hierarchy-query fault on POST /api/spe/configs and on PUT returns the same 503 with no `CreateAsync` / `UpdateAsync`. `GET /api/spe/configs` (list) keeps its existing 500 on the same fault. Seeding proof: restoring the old `return true` makes the five-route test fail.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Non-admin on the six /api/spe routes: a signed-in caller with no Admin/SystemAdmin role still gets 403 with reasonCode "sdap.access.deny.role_insufficient" on each (layer 1 unchanged), and an anonymous caller gets 401.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Regression: `SpeAdminContainerItemRouteGateTests`, `SpeAdminAuthorizationLayerTests`, `TenantScopeHierarchyTests`, `BulkOperationTests`, `SpeAdminClientRouteAgreementTests`, `SpeWriteSinkContainerProvenanceGuardTests` and `Phase2RecordMatchingTests` stay green; one representative other /api/spe route that passes `?configId=` (for example `GET /api/spe/containers?configId=`) still reaches its handler for an in-scope admin, through the host.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Route guard: (a) if task 167 has landed on this branch — no Pending waiver owned by "165" remains for any of the nine routes (POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync, POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync-incremental, GET /api/admin/record-matching/status, GET/PUT/DELETE /api/spe/configs/{id} — whose template is now {configId:guid}, so a waiver keyed on the old template must be deleted, not renamed — POST /api/spe/configs, POST /api/spe/bulk/delete, POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions), and each route passes the guard's per-resource rule; (b) if 167 has not landed — `Api/SpeAdmin/ConfigEndpoints.cs` and `Api/SpeAdmin/BulkOperationEndpoints.cs` are added to GovernedFiles as `Scope.GroupGated` with a reason citing this task and the new test class, `RecordMatchingAdminEndpoints.cs` is left for 167 to classify, and the task note and PR say "167 not landed; main session reconciles waivers". In both cases every RouteAuthorizationGuardTests test passes, and no Permanent waiver is added for any of the nine routes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Seeding proofs recorded in the task note, one per guard, each naming the test that failed: (1) group policy reverted to bare RequireAuthorization(); (2) route parameter renamed back to `{id:guid}`; (3) filter body-argument read removed; (4) disagreeing-configId 400 removed (both the config-route and the bulk case); (5) does-not-exist 404 removed (filter passes unknown configs again); (6) unverifiable 503 reverted to allow; (7) POST business-unit intersection removed; (8) PUT business-unit intersection removed; (9) app-identity check removed from POST, and separately from PUT; (10) per-item container-type call removed from each job path.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: tests cover exactly the behaviours above and the fail-closed branches named in the constraints. Any additional test carries a one-line justification in the task note. No Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;, no DI-registration test, no constructor null-check test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">BFF build green; affected tests, full BFF unit suite, ArchTests, and BOTH integration suites (`tests/integration/Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests`, `tests/integration/Spe.Integration.Tests`) run in full and green; publish size reported against a fresh origin/master build per CLAUDE.md §10 (both absolute sizes, delta, zip tool, equal file counts), ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="false">MANUAL LIVE GATE (dev, run by the main session after deploy; ids redacted to 8 chars in the note): (a) if `DocumentIntelligence__RecordMatchingEnabled` is true in dev, a non-admin test user gets 403 on GET /api/admin/record-matching/status and on POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync (refused, so no side effect), and an admin gets 200 on GET /status; if the flag is false, record that and skip (a). (b) An SPE admin whose business unit excludes config X's business unit gets 404 on GET /api/spe/configs/{X}, byte-identical apart from trace id to GET on a random GUID. (c) The same admin's PUT, DELETE, bulk/delete and bulk/permissions on X are refused with the same 404, and a POST /api/spe/configs naming X's containerTypeId in the admin's own unit is refused with 403 — run these WRITE probes only with the owner's approval and only against a throwaway config and throwaway containers, so a failed gate destroys nothing. (d) An in-scope admin can still list, open and edit their own config in the SpeAdminApp and start a bulk delete on a throwaway container. (e) Read-only, recorded during development (step 0): the count of `sprk_specontainertypeconfig` rows with no business unit, the business units that hold configs, the configs in different leaf units sharing any of the five identity fields, and whether dev has an SPE admin outside the root business unit. If no such admin exists, ask the owner to create one (owner round 4 item 1) rather than moving an existing user.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Sweep findings closed here, each by a criterion naming its route: #44 POST /api/spe/bulk/delete (high), #45 PUT /api/spe/configs/{id} (high), #47 POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync (medium), #48 POST /api/admin/record-matching/sync-incremental (medium), #72 POST /api/spe/bulk/permissions (medium), #73 GET /api/spe/configs/{id} (medium), #74 POST /api/spe/configs (medium: business unit AND the borrowed container type / app identity), #75 DELETE /api/spe/configs/{id} (medium), #77 GET /api/admin/record-matching/status (low). Is the admin policy appropriate where an operator route still acts on a caller-chosen record? For record-matching, yes: no record id is chosen, so the "SystemAdmin" policy is the whole decision. For the /api/spe routes, the admin role gate (layer 1, equivalent to the policy's role branch) is kept, and it is NOT enough by itself, because the codebase defines business-unit scope as the boundary between admins (`SpeAdminTenantScope` remarks: "a customer administrator sits in a leaf unit"); that is why the tenant-scope filter must run on every one of them and why create/update must not borrow another unit's identity. NOT in this task (sweep "Noteworthy", not among the 82; for the main session to file): GET /api/spe/bulk/{operationId}/status is not bound to the caller who started the operation; environment routes (EnvironmentEndpoints.cs) and dashboard routes have no business-unit scoping, and PUT/POST configs can still link any `environmentId`; container, item and permission routes check the config's business unit but not that the container belongs to it; the "SystemAdmin" policy's scope-substring branch (`AuthorizationModule.cs:372-374`); POST /api/spe/containertypes/{typeId}/register sends an app-only token to a caller-supplied host; `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.ClaimOnlyFilters["SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter"]` says it decides "from claims", which is inaccurate (it reads Dataverse through SpeAdminTenantScope).</notes>

